---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const REPORT_EXAMPLE = `{
  "exitCode": 1,
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "/work/config.json",
      "opened": true,
      "saved": true,
      "valid": false,
      "errorCount": 1,
      "errors": [{ "pointer": "/retries", "message": "\\"3\\" is not of type \\"integer\\"" }],
      "problem": null
    }
  ]
}`;

const SHAPE_EXAMPLE = `{
  "path": "$",
  "kind": "object",
  "count": 2,
  "bytes": 206444476,
  "shape": {
    "items": [{ "email?": "string", "id": "integer", "name": "string", "tags": ["string"] }],
    "meta": { "v": "integer" }
  }
}`;
---
<DocsLayout
  title="Command line"
  description="Open, pipe and compare JSON from any terminal with the pandia command, query, validate, diff and convert files of any size without a window, review piped JSON for an AI agent, and use Pandia as git's diff tool."
  currentDoc="/docs/command-line"
>
  <h1>Command line</h1>
  <p class="lead">Open, pipe and compare JSON from any terminal with the <code>pandia</code> command.</p>

  <h2>Install the command</h2>
  <p>Open <strong>Settings → Behavior → terminal command</strong> and click
  <strong>Install pandia command</strong>. Then open a new terminal and run
  <code>pandia --help</code>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>macOS</strong> creates <code>/usr/local/bin/pandia</code>. macOS may ask for your
    password. Homebrew installs the command with the app; if <code>pandia</code> isn't found after a
    Homebrew install, run <code>brew reinstall --cask pandia</code> once.</li>
    <li><strong>Windows</strong> creates <code>pandia.cmd</code> in Pandia's data folder and adds
    that folder to your user PATH.</li>
    <li><strong>Linux</strong> creates <code>~/.local/bin/pandia</code>. If your shell can't find it,
    add <code>~/.local/bin</code> to your PATH.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Open files</h2>
  <pre><code><span class="k">pandia</span> data.json
<span class="k">pandia</span> a.json b.json
<span class="k">curl</span> -s https://api.example.com/users | <span class="k">pandia</span> -
<span class="k">pandia</span> --diff old.json new.json
<span class="k">pandia</span> config.json --schema config.schema.json</code></pre>
  <p>If Pandia is already open, each file opens as a new tab in that window. The command returns
  straight away, so your terminal stays free.</p>
  <p><code>-</code> opens whatever is piped in, in a tab named <strong>stdin</strong>. While the tab
  is open, Pandia keeps a temp copy that only your account can read, and deletes it when you close
  the tab. Save asks where to keep it. YAML, CSV and broken JSON are converted or repaired the same
  way as files you open.</p>
  <p><code>--diff</code> opens the first file in <a href="/docs/compare">Compare</a> against the
  second. <code>--schema</code> fills the Schema panel for every file the command opens; see
  <a href="/docs/validate">Validate</a>.</p>

  <h2>Wait for Pandia</h2>
  <p><code>--wait</code> keeps the command running until you close every tab it opened. Its exit
  code tells a script what happened:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>0</td><td>You closed the tabs and the Schema panel showed no errors.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1</td><td>The Schema panel showed errors, a file didn't open, or Pandia quit before you closed the tabs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td>The command was mistyped. Pandia prints what to fix.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3</td><td>Piped input was closed without saving, so nothing was sent back.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <pre><code><span class="k">pandia</span> --wait config.json --schema config.schema.json && <span class="k">echo</span> "config is valid"</code></pre>

  <h2>Review piped JSON and send it back</h2>
  <p>With <code>--wait</code>, piped input becomes a review step for a script or an AI agent. A
  bar in the tab says a terminal is waiting. Edit the JSON, press <strong>Save</strong>
  (<kbd>⌘S</kbd>) to send it back, then close the tab. The command prints the saved JSON.</p>
  <pre><code><span class="k">cat</span> draft.json | <span class="k">pandia</span> --wait - --schema config.schema.json > config.next.json && <span class="k">mv</span> config.next.json config.json</code></pre>
  <p>Closing the tab without saving sends nothing and exits with 3, so <code>&&</code> stops the
  rest of the script. Write to a new file as above: <code>></code> empties its target as soon as the
  command starts, so redirecting straight into <code>config.json</code> would leave it empty.</p>

  <h2>Get a report your script can read</h2>
  <p>Add <code>--json</code> to <code>--wait</code> and the command prints one JSON report when the
  tabs close, instead of the document:</p>
  <pre><code>{REPORT_EXAMPLE}</code></pre>
  <p>Each error names its location as a JSON Pointer. <code>valid</code> is <code>null</code> when no
  schema ran. <code>problem</code> says why a file didn't open or why its schema couldn't be used.
  Piped input has a <code>path</code> of <code>null</code>, and once saved, its JSON is in
  <code>content</code>.</p>

  <h2>Answer in the terminal, without a window</h2>
  <p>These commands read a file, print the answer and exit. No window opens, so scripts and AI agents
  can use them on their own, on files of any size. Use <code>-</code> as the file to read what is
  piped in. YAML, XML and CSV are converted first, like files you open.</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Command</th><th>What it prints</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>pandia get &lt;file&gt; &lt;path&gt;</code></td><td>The value at a path, as JSON. Write the path the way Pandia shows it, like <code>$.items[0].name</code>, or as a JSON Pointer, like <code>/items/0/name</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>pandia shape &lt;file&gt; [path]</code></td><td>The kind, item count and a sketch of the types, without the data.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>pandia validate &lt;file&gt; --schema &lt;s&gt;</code></td><td>Whether the file matches a JSON Schema, with each error's JSON Pointer.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>pandia diff &lt;a&gt; &lt;b&gt;</code></td><td>Each value added, removed, changed or moved, with its path and its values before and after.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>pandia search &lt;file&gt; &lt;text&gt;</code></td><td>The path of every key or value that contains the text.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>pandia types &lt;file&gt; --lang ts</code></td><td>Types, like the Types panel: <code>ts</code>, <code>zod</code>, <code>rust</code>, <code>go</code>, <code>python</code>, <code>java</code>, <code>kotlin</code>, <code>php</code>, <code>dart</code> or <code>json-schema</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>pandia convert &lt;file&gt; --to yaml</code></td><td>The document as <code>json</code>, <code>json-min</code>, <code>yaml</code>, <code>csv</code> or <code>xml</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>pandia repair &lt;file&gt;</code></td><td>Broken JSON fixed, with each fix listed on stderr.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <pre><code><span class="k">pandia</span> shape events.json
<span class="k">pandia</span> get events.json '$.items[1999999].email'
<span class="k">curl</span> -s https://api.example.com/users | <span class="k">pandia</span> validate - --schema users.schema.json</code></pre>
  <p><code>shape</code> shows a large file's structure without printing it. A key ending in
  <code>?</code> is missing from some items:</p>
  <pre><code>{SHAPE_EXAMPLE}</code></pre>
  <p>Put a path in single quotes so the shell doesn't read <code>$</code> or <code>[</code>.
  <code>pandia &lt;command&gt; --help</code> shows a command's options. The exit code works like
  <code>diff</code> and <code>grep</code>:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>0</td><td>Done: found, valid, or the same.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1</td><td>The answer is no: the path doesn't exist, the file is invalid, the files differ, or nothing matched. For a missing path, stderr says where it stops, like <code>$.items has 2 items, so the last is $.items[1]</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td>The command was mistyped, or the file can't be read. Pandia prints what to fix.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>To open a file whose name is a command, like <code>get</code>, write <code>pandia -- get</code>.</p>

  <h2>Use Pandia as git's diff tool</h2>
  <pre><code><span class="k">git</span> config --global difftool.pandia.cmd 'pandia --wait --diff "$LOCAL" "$REMOTE"'
<span class="k">git</span> difftool --tool=pandia -- data.json</code></pre>
  <p>On Windows, write <code>pandia.cmd</code> instead of <code>pandia</code> in the first line.</p>

  <h2>Options</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Option</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>-</code></td><td>Open what is piped in.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--schema &lt;path|url&gt;</code></td><td>Validate the opened files against this schema.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--diff &lt;a&gt; &lt;b&gt;</code></td><td>Open <code>a</code> in Compare against <code>b</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--wait</code></td><td>Return after you close the opened tabs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--json</code></td><td>With <code>--wait</code>, print a JSON report of what happened.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>-h</code>, <code>--help</code></td><td>Show the options.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>-v</code>, <code>--version</code></td><td>Show the installed version, like <code>pandia 1.0.8</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--</code></td><td>Treat every name after it as a file, like <code>pandia -- -data.json</code>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</DocsLayout>
